.TH zterm "" "Command"
.SH NAME
zterm \- GUI terminal window
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBzterm [\fIoptions\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
zterm -- GUI terminal window
Usage:	zterm [ options ]
	Runs an interactive shell in an 80x25 window with 200
	lines of scrollback.  Arrow keys send ^P ^N ^B ^F.
.HE
.B zterm
opens an 80x25-character terminal window under the
.B zview
desktop and runs an interactive
.B /bin/sh
in it on a pseudo-terminal, with full job control: ^C interrupts,
^\e quits, and the line discipline is the normal tty one.
The window closes by itself when the shell exits.
Quitting the window from its menu asks for confirmation first, then
hangs up the shell.
.B zterm
can be started from a desktop icon or typed at a shell inside
another
.BR zterm ;
it accepts the standard window
.I options
of GUI applications (though not
.BR \-S :
the size is fixed); see
.BR zview .
.PP
Keystrokes go to the shell.
The hi-res keyboard delivers plain ASCII, and its special keys
produce control codes rather than escape sequences:
.DS
.ta 6n 24n
	\fBUp, Down\fR	^P, ^N
	\fBLeft, Right\fR	^B, ^F
	\fBHOME, END\fR	^A, ^E
	\fBPG UP, PG DN\fR	ESC v, ^V (M-v page up in \fBme\fR)
	\fBBS\fR	^H (0x08)
	\fBDEL, keypad Del\fR	DEL (0x7f)
	\fBCtrl+arrows\fR	^A ^E ESC-v ^V (= Home, End, PG UP, PG DN)
	\fBShift+arrows\fR	ESC b/f/</> (word back/fwd, buffer top/end)
	\fBF1\fR	^@ (set mark in \fBme\fR)
	\fBF2\fR	^X ^S (save in \fBme\fR)
	\fBF3\fR	^X ^V (visit a file in \fBme\fR)
	\fBF4\fR	^R (reverse search in \fBme\fR)
	\fBF5\fR	ESC w (copy region in \fBme\fR)
	\fBF6\fR	^W (kill region in \fBme\fR)
	\fBF7\fR	^S (search in \fBme\fR)
	\fBF8\fR	^K ^K (kill to end of line + newline in \fBme\fR)
	\fBF9\fR	^Y (yank in \fBme\fR)
	\fBF10\fR	^X ^C (quit in \fBme\fR)
	\fBShift+F1\fR	ESC ! (reposition in \fBme\fR)
	\fBShift+F2\fR	^X ^W (write named file in \fBme\fR)
	\fBShift+F3\fR	^X b (use/create a buffer in \fBme\fR)
	\fBShift+F4\fR	^X ^X (swap mark and cursor in \fBme\fR)
	\fBShift+F5\fR	^X ^U (region to upper case in \fBme\fR)
	\fBShift+F6\fR	^X ^L (region to lower case in \fBme\fR)
	\fBShift+F7\fR	^R (search backward in \fBme\fR)
	\fBShift+F8\fR	ESC d (delete word in \fBme\fR)
	\fBShift+F9\fR	^X ( (begin keyboard macro in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCtrl+F1\fR	ESC c (capitalise word in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCtrl+F2\fR	^X ^F (set file name in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCtrl+F3\fR	^X ^R (read a file in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCtrl+F4\fR	ESC u (word to upper case in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCtrl+F5\fR	^X 2 (split window in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCtrl+F6\fR	^X 1 (one window in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCtrl+F7\fR	ESC l (word to lower case in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCtrl+F8\fR	ESC ^H (delete word back in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCtrl+F9\fR	^X ) (end keyboard macro in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCtrl+F10\fR	^Z (save all and exit in \fBme\fR)
	\fBCLR/HOME\fR	ESC < (top of buffer; shifted, ESC > the end;
		Ctrl, ^X k kill buffer)
	\fBPOP/PUSH\fR	^X n (next window; shifted, ^X p previous;
		Ctrl, ^X z enlarge)
	\fBSCRN/PRT\fR	^L (redraw; shifted, ^X ^Z shrink window;
		Ctrl, ^X = show position)
	\fBSTOP/CONT\fR	^G (abort; shifted, ^X e execute macro;
		Ctrl, ^X ^B list buffers)
.DE
The F-keys follow the Norton Commander editor's bar (F1 = mark,
F3 = open, F4 = replace, F9 = paste) as
.BR me 's
own key bindings, so mark/copy/move/delete blocks, search, save and
quit read the same in
.B me
here as the function keys do in
.BR zedit ,
and the kernel consoles send the identical bytes.
Beware that at a shell prompt ^S stops terminal output until ^Q is
typed \- the tty's own flow control.
The remaining function keys produce codes with no byte value and are
ignored in a terminal window.
.PP
On the output side
.B zterm
emulates a deliberate subset of the ANSI terminal, enough for the
shell, simple full-screen programs and
.BR ls :
CR, LF, BS, TAB (8-column stops), and the escape sequences
.B "ESC [ r;cH"
(and
.BR f )
cursor position,
.B "ESC [ nA/B/C/D"
cursor motion,
.B "ESC [ J"
clear screen and home, and
.B "ESC [ K"
clear to end of line.
There are no character attributes: bold, reverse and colour are not
rendered.
Other escape sequences are swallowed silently.
.PP
Up to 200 lines that scroll off the top are kept and can be viewed
with the scrollbar on the left edge; any shell output snaps the view
back to the live screen.
A left drag selects text (wrapped lines are joined, trailing blanks
trimmed) and publishes it as the PRIMARY selection on release; a
middle click in any terminal pastes the PRIMARY selection to the
shell.
The window menu's
.B Copy
copies the PRIMARY selection to the clipboard, and
.B Paste
types the clipboard to the shell.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 8n 36n
	/dev/ptyp0 ... /dev/ptyp7	pseudo-terminals
	/usr/hr/bin/hrpump	I/O helper (internal)
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR zview ,
.BR sh ,
.B stty
.SH NOTES
At most 8 terminal windows can exist at once \- one pseudo-terminal
pair each.
The shell is
.B /bin/sh
regardless of environment.
A paste is limited to 1024 bytes, and the tty driver itself accepts
at most 255 characters in one input line.
The scrollback is dropped, oldest first, when memory runs short.
.B hrpump
is
.BR zterm 's
internal I/O helper and does nothing useful run by hand.
